    question: 'How often should we be doing this?',
    answer:
      'Most workplaces land on two extraction cleans a year with spot treatment in between as things happen. A busy reception, a clinic or a venue often needs quarterly. A quiet upstairs office can stretch to annually. It comes down to foot traffic rather than floor area, and a walkthrough settles it quickly.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      'We come and look at the carpet, test the fibre and tell you what will lift and what will not. Then you get a written scope and a price, with the after hours option shown separately. No obligation. If it suits, we book a night that works around your trading.',
  },
];

// §12 Non-branded FAQs. The long-tail block, aimed at what people search
// rather than what they ask us.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. These claims are checkable:
//   - Hot water extraction is the method most carpet manufacturers specify in
//     their maintenance requirements. Correct in substance. The interval
//     varies by brand, which is why none is stated.
//   - Bonnet cleaning can void some carpet warranties. Correct for several
//     major makers. Hedged to "some" and "can", deliberately.
//   - Cleaning Services Award 2020 (MA000022) penalty rates for weekends and
//     late shifts. Correct, and consistent with the hub, which says the same.
//   - Drying time is NOT given as a number anywhere in this block, because
//     the honest answer depends on equipment TLB has not specified.
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who prices carpet work, and a
// visible "last reviewed" date, since the Award changes every 1 July.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How long does commercial carpet take to dry?',
    answer:
      'A properly extracted carpet is usually walkable within a few hours and fully dry inside the day, which is why the work is done overnight. Drying comes down to three things: how much water went in, how much air is moving across the floor, and the humidity. Air movers cut it substantially. A carpet still damp the next morning was over-wet, not thoroughly cleaned.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between hot water extraction and encapsulation?',
    answer:
      'Hot water extraction injects heated water and solution into the pile under pressure and vacuums it back out with the soil. It removes the most and takes the longest to dry. Encapsulation uses a polymer that crystallises around the soil so it can be vacuumed out the next day. It is faster and drier but lighter, so it suits interim maintenance between extraction cleans rather than replacing them.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does carpet cleaning damage the carpet?',
    answer:
      'Done properly, no. Done badly, yes. Carpet gets damaged two ways: over-wetting, which leaves moisture in the backing and can cause odour, delamination or mould, and using the wrong chemistry for the fibre, which is how wool ends up bleached or browned. That is why a competent cleaner tests the fibre in a hidden spot before starting instead of treating every floor the same.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can carpet cleaning void a warranty?',
    answer:
      'It can. Many carpet manufacturers set maintenance requirements inside the warranty, and most of those specify periodic hot water extraction by a professional. Some exclude bonnet cleaning outright. If your building still has its carpet warranty, check what the maker requires before you accept a quote, and ask the cleaner in writing whether their method meets it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does commercial carpet cleaning cost?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE. The most searched question on the topic, and
      // the page cannot answer it without a number from TLB. A "from $X per
      // square metre" that turns out to be wrong is worse than no number, and
      // the ACCC treats from-pricing as a claim.
      'Commercial carpet is usually priced per square metre, with a minimum charge for small jobs, and the rate moves with the condition of the floor, how much furniture there is to work around and whether the work is after hours. Ask for the price against a written scope rather than an hourly rate, so you can see what is actually included. [TLB’s pricing basis and minimum charge. Confirm.]',
  },
];

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Commercial carpet cleaning',
  heading: 'Start with a walkthrough.',
  lead: 'We come and look at the carpet, test the fibre and tell you what will lift and what will not. Then you get a written scope and a price. No obligation.',
  // 🔴 This is the clearest place the conflict shows. The heading says start
  // with a walkthrough and the button underneath says book a night clean.
  cta: { label: primaryCta.label, href: quoteCta.href },
};
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      subheading={hero.subheading}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid
        across both sections. 75% keeps Dark Teal body copy above 4.5:1 over
        the darkest part of the frame. Same treatment as the hub. */}
    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={pathwayCards.heading} headingAccent={pathwayCards.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards.cards} />

    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="icon-grid"
      heading="What our commercial carpet cleaning services involve, stage by stage"
      lead="Six stages, in this order. Skip the first two and the rest is just wet carpet."
      items={scopeItems}
      cta={{ label: primaryCta.label, href: quoteCta.href }}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading="What a low carpet quote usually leaves out"
      body={worthKnowingBody}
    />

    {/* §17. New H2, per the brief. `light` between the dark "low quote"
        band above and TagCloud's brand fill below, so the page keeps
        alternating rather than stacking three filled bands. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading="Office carpet cleaning services, worked around a locked building"
      body={officeBody}
    />

    {/* ⚠️ RESERVED SLOT: before and after slider (BeforeAfter.astro).

        Carpet is the best before/after subject on this site and the component
        is already running on five pages, so this section would do more here
        than any paragraph above it.

        NOT BUILT because BeforeAfter has no reserved-slot mode by design: its
        `src` is required, since a comparison slider with nothing to compare
        has no meaningful empty state. It needs real paired photography and
        none exists for commercial work.

        THE PHOTO BRIEF: one traffic lane or corridor, shot from a fixed
        tripod position, before and after, with NOTHING moved between the two
        frames. A shifted camera or a chair pushed in reads as a glitch rather
        than as cleaning. Two or three pairs is plenty. Drop it in here,
        between "Worth knowing" and the related pages. */}

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we work, and the premises we cover"
      subheading="Commercial carpet cleaning for workplaces across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      tags={settingTags}
      note={[]}
    />

    <ComparisonTable
      heading={comparisonTable.heading}
      cornerLabel={comparisonTable.cornerLabel}
      lead={comparisonTable.lead}
      columns={comparisonTable.columns}
      rows={comparisonTable.rows}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What our clients say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading={footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.fast}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial]}
    />

    {/* Brief §23 and §24. The page carried two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error. This block is about
        working with TLB, the next is about the service, and naming them that
        way makes both useful. Title Case also breaks BFD §9.2. */}
    <Faq heading="Booking a commercial carpet clean" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Commercial carpet cleaning questions" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
